may is mental health awareness month and  we're having important conversations  about mental health every monday this  month one in five americans will  experience mental illness in any given  year  and one in five children struggle with  mental health but finding and paying for  mental health care can be challenging  and overwhelming research shows that  only 46 percent of adults with mental  illness received health care for it  jamie howard is a senior clinical  psychologist from the child mind  institute jamie good morning how you  doing good morning good thank you um so  there are many different types of mental  health care um where what are the  different types and then where do you  start right okay so the term therapist  can be confusing because it refers to a  wide array of professionals so one is a  licensed clinical social worker that you  might hear of this is someone who has a  master's degree with specialization in  providing therapy then there's a  clinical psychologist like myself  and the letters you might see are phd or  psyd and we specialize in providing  therapy as well we also do research and  provide testing and then there's  psychiatrists and they have mds they go  to medical school and they usually tend  to focus on prescribing medicine so  there's all of these different  professionals who can provide mental  health care one of the most important  things you want to look for is what we  call evidence-based  mental health care so that means that  there's research to support the various  elements of the therapy that your  provider is administering and one of the  most important things is to remember  regardless of what type of mental health  care provider you have you want them to  be licensed and credentialed okay so so  how do you know as a parent when it's  time to seek out professional help right  so there's a few things we want to keep  in mind when you're monitoring your  child's mood or behavior you want to  think about the duration how long has  this been going on for if it's been up  to a month or so that's when we start to  think that treatment might be indicated  and you should at least get an  evaluation  you also want to consider the intensity  so is this symptom so for example you  have a very irritable teenager is this  like any other teenager are they just  kind of difficult right now or is this  more significant you can sort of get a  sense by talking to other parents or  teachers pediatrician to get a sense of  that then you also want to look at the  frequency if it's happening most days  the mood or behavior problem that you're  noticing then it's also a good  indication to get treatment some people  may make a call and say all right it's  time whether it's for myself as an adult  or my family members my kids but then  they can be booked up for months at a  time right um so what do you do in that  situation so one thing we like to  recommend is that you start with either  your general practitioner or your  pediatrician because sometimes they can  actually treat the medical issues  underlying a psychological condition and  then if not they also usually have  really good community resources to refer  you to they might sort of have their  finger on the pulse of who's available  and where's a good place to go the other  thing to keep in mind is telehealth  so  it's sort of the silver lining of the  pandemic in so many ways because it's  reduced a lot of barriers to care for  families for adults and children alike  so i still do a lot of telehealth in my  practice even though we're able to meet  in person people just tend to prefer it  because you don't have to deal with  parking and transportation it just cuts  down on a lot of hassle jamie you're the  best thank you so much thank you i  appreciate you
 

